# pgBackRest R2 credentials secret (P1.1).
#
# DO NOT COMMIT A FILLED-IN COPY OF THIS FILE. Apply via operator action:
#
#   1. Create the R2 API token:
#      - Cloudflare dashboard → R2 → Manage API Tokens → Create
#      - Permission: "Object Read & Write"
#      - Bucket scope: enclii-backups (or "All buckets" for org-wide use)
#      - Copy Access Key ID + Secret Access Key (shown once)
#
#   2. (Optional) Generate a cipher passphrase for client-side AES-256-CBC
#      encryption of archives. Adds CPU cost; R2 is already encrypted at rest
#      by Cloudflare. **Operator decision** — pick one:
#
#      WITHOUT client-side encryption (simpler, relies on R2 server-side enc):
#        - In pgbackrest-config.yaml, change `repo1-cipher-type=aes-256-cbc` to
#          `repo1-cipher-type=none` and delete the cipher-pass line in that
#          ConfigMap.
#        - Omit PGBACKREST_REPO1_CIPHER_PASS below.
#
#      WITH client-side encryption (recommended for regulated-compliance
#      customers like Karafiel; keeps ciphertext-only copies in R2):
#        - Keep repo1-cipher-type=aes-256-cbc.
#        - Generate a 64-char urandom passphrase:
#            openssl rand -base64 48 | tr -d '\n' > /tmp/pgbackrest-cipher.txt
#        - Back up the passphrase to Vault (Selva RFC 0005 tooling) BEFORE
#          the first backup runs. A lost passphrase = all archives become
#          unreadable. Treat it like a KMS root key.
#
#   3. Apply (replace placeholder values, do not commit):
#
#      kubectl create secret generic pgbackrest-r2-credentials \
#        --namespace=data \
#        --from-literal=R2_ACCOUNT_ID='<your-cf-account-id>' \
#        --from-literal=PGBACKREST_REPO1_S3_KEY='<r2-access-key-id>' \
#        --from-literal=PGBACKREST_REPO1_S3_KEY_SECRET='<r2-secret-key>' \
#        --from-literal=PGBACKREST_REPO1_CIPHER_PASS="$(cat /tmp/pgbackrest-cipher.txt)"
#
#      shred /tmp/pgbackrest-cipher.txt
#
#   4. Verify (should list keys without values):
#
#      kubectl get secret pgbackrest-r2-credentials -n data -o jsonpath='{.data}' | jq 'keys'
#
# Expected keys:
#   - R2_ACCOUNT_ID                        (subdomain for the R2 endpoint URL)
#   - PGBACKREST_REPO1_S3_KEY              (AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID equivalent)
#   - PGBACKREST_REPO1_S3_KEY_SECRET       (AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY equivalent)
#   - PGBACKREST_REPO1_CIPHER_PASS         (optional; omit if cipher-type=none)
#
# pgBackRest recognizes env vars named PGBACKREST_<OPTION_UPPER> natively, so
# we do not need a separate config layer to wire these in.
#
# Migration path to External Secrets (future P1.1 follow-up):
#   Once Vault is operational (P0.2 complete), rotate these into Vault and
#   replace this one-shot secret with an ExternalSecret manifest. The naming
#   here is chosen to map 1:1 onto a Vault KV-v2 path like
#   `secret/data/enclii/pgbackrest-r2` so the ESO spec is trivial.
---
# Reference — do not apply this unmodified. It contains placeholder values
# that are explicitly banned from the .env/secret-file protocol.
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: pgbackrest-r2-credentials
  namespace: data
  labels:
    app.kubernetes.io/name: pgbackrest
    app.kubernetes.io/component: backup
    app.kubernetes.io/part-of: enclii
type: Opaque
stringData:
  R2_ACCOUNT_ID: "example-local-cloudflare-account-id"
  PGBACKREST_REPO1_S3_KEY: "example-local-r2-access-key-id"
  PGBACKREST_REPO1_S3_KEY_SECRET: "example-local-r2-secret-access-key"
  PGBACKREST_REPO1_CIPHER_PASS: "example-local-cipher-passphrase"
